pg-agent-rs — third-party notices
=================================

pg-agent-rs itself is licensed under either of Apache License 2.0
(LICENSE-APACHE) or the MIT license (LICENSE-MIT), at your option.

This file is about the *other* code a pg_agentd binary contains. A source
checkout of this repository contains none of it — the crates below are
fetched from crates.io at build time — so this matters when you distribute
the `.deb` or the `.rpm`, not when you clone the repo.

The list is the Linux dependency graph with every feature enabled, which is
the largest a pg-agent-rs build gets: 252 third-party crates, of which 240
are satisfiable by taking the MIT option alone. The remaining 12 are named
below, because each carries a term the MIT/Apache pair does not.

Nothing here conflicts with releasing pg-agent-rs under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
There is no GPL-, AGPL-, or LGPL-only dependency in the graph, and — unlike
most server software of this shape — no vendored C or C++ library beyond
ring's. That is not an accident; see "Native code" below.


Regenerating this list, and the caveat
--------------------------------------

    cargo deny check licenses          # the CI gate; policy in deny.toml
    cargo tree -e normal --all-features \
        --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu \
        --prefix none --format '{l}|{p}' | sort -u

This file is a human-scale summary, not a substitute for shipping license
texts. MIT and the BSD licenses require the copyright notice and permission
text to travel with a binary, and reproducing 252 of those here would go
stale on the first `cargo update`. Generate the full bundle at release time:

    cargo about generate about.hbs > THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html


ISC
---

    ring                 Apache-2.0 AND ISC   https://github.com/briansmith/ring
    rustls-webpki        ISC                  https://github.com/rustls/webpki
    untrusted            ISC                  https://github.com/briansmith/untrusted

`ring` is an AND, not a choice: both Apache-2.0 and ISC terms apply. It is
the sole rustls crypto provider, and deliberately so — the workspace
Cargo.toml explains at length why aws-lc-rs is kept out of the graph.


BSD licenses
------------

    subtle               BSD-3-Clause         https://github.com/dalek-cryptography/subtle
    matchit              MIT AND BSD-3-Clause https://github.com/ibraheemdev/matchit

`matchit` is an AND: axum's router carries both sets of terms.


Unicode License v3
------------------

    unicode-ident        (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND Unicode-3.0
                         https://github.com/dtolnay/unicode-ident

The Unicode half is required, not optional — the crate embeds Unicode
character tables, and Unicode-3.0's one substantive requirement is that
this notice accompany them. It reaches the graph through `syn`, i.e.
through every derive macro in the tree.


No MIT option
-------------

These offer no MIT arm, so a pg-agent-rs binary is distributed with at
least one of them under Apache-2.0. Apache-2.0 §4(d) propagates any NOTICE
file the upstream ships, which is part of why this file exists.

    prost                Apache-2.0    protobuf runtime, via tonic
    prost-derive         Apache-2.0
    prost-types          Apache-2.0
    anyerror             Apache-2.0    via openraft
    sync_wrapper         Apache-2.0    via tower/axum
    ryu                  Apache-2.0 OR BSL-1.0


Native code
-----------

Worth stating explicitly, because it is a property the packaging depends on
and one a dependency bump could silently end: **ring's C and assembly are
the only native code in this build.** Everything else is pure Rust.

That is what makes `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` tractable, and the static
musl binary is in turn what lets one `.deb` and one `.rpm` install on
Debian 12, Ubuntu 24.04, Rocky 9 and Alpine alike (see
`scripts/build-pkgs.sh` and testing/README.md finding 26). A dependency
that vendors a C++ library — RocksDB and its five compression backends are
the canonical example, and are why saltator cannot do this — would take
that away, and would also add a body of license terms that `cargo deny`
structurally cannot see, since it reads crate manifests and not vendored
sources.

So: if a future dependency brings native code, this file and the packaging
story both need revisiting, and neither the license gate nor the build will
tell you on its own. The release workflow's "Assert the binary is static"
step is the one thing that would notice.


What is not covered here
------------------------

Build-time-only dependencies are excluded (`cargo tree -e normal`), because
they do not ship in the binary: `protoc-bin-vendored` and its per-platform
protoc binaries, `tonic-build`, and the proc-macro crates' build machinery.
If pg-agent-rs ever ships a source bundle rather than a binary, that
exclusion stops being safe.
